WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.876
- They like, you know,
you gon' be a top-20,

00:00:01.876 --> 00:00:03.003
whatever, whatever.

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:06.006
So I had great workouts, great meetings.

00:00:06.006 --> 00:00:08.091
People calling me all the way up

00:00:08.091 --> 00:00:09.676
until the draft date.

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:11.302
So when I get there and
it's like, aight,

00:00:11.302 --> 00:00:12.637
I'm still waiting, still waiting.

00:00:12.637 --> 00:00:16.391
It's like, the way I was
waiting for that long,

00:00:16.391 --> 00:00:19.686
it's like, aight. You know,
you gon' pay for that.

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:21.229
You gon' pay for that for sure.
♪♪♪

00:00:21.229 --> 00:00:23.398
- [Announcer:] Heidegger kicks
it out to Sharife Cooper,

00:00:23.398 --> 00:00:24.315
who has the shot

00:00:24.315 --> 00:00:25.775
and he nails it!

00:00:25.775 --> 00:00:29.279
Sharife Cooper for three, wins the game!

00:00:30.947 --> 00:00:35.035
- [Eric Yeboah:] So first question is,
from AOT, McEachern, Jersey,

00:00:35.035 --> 00:00:36.703
your path, you finally got now

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:38.913
to your first NBA media training camp.

00:00:38.913 --> 00:00:40.248
What does it feel like?

00:00:40.248 --> 00:00:41.583
- Oh, it's still surreal.

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:44.044
I don't think I've taken it in yet.

00:00:44.044 --> 00:00:46.379
Like, I just feel like once you get,
you know, to this level,

00:00:46.379 --> 00:00:49.299
it's like, you got so much
more that you want to do

00:00:49.299 --> 00:00:51.593
and accomplish, it's
like, you can't really,

00:00:51.593 --> 00:00:53.887
you know, take a step back
and be like, all right,

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:54.929
I made it.

00:00:54.929 --> 00:00:56.973
- I think patience is the first word

00:00:56.973 --> 00:00:58.308
that comes to mind for you.

00:00:59.350 --> 00:01:02.604
Last season, you had to be
patient to finally start playing.

00:01:02.604 --> 00:01:03.438
Now, what is it?

00:01:03.438 --> 00:01:05.356
What does the word patience mean to you?

00:01:05.356 --> 00:01:06.441
- Patience, sacrifice.

00:01:06.441 --> 00:01:09.986
Those are two words I've
been, you know, raised up on.

00:01:09.986 --> 00:01:12.655
So, I mentioned that I know
something, nothing that,

00:01:12.655 --> 00:01:14.074
you know, kind of rattle me.

00:01:15.283 --> 00:01:17.285
You know, it's hard when
you finally get to a point

00:01:17.285 --> 00:01:19.746
and then stuff just randomly happen

00:01:19.746 --> 00:01:22.123
that you had no clue of
or no control over.

00:01:22.123 --> 00:01:25.126
So that's probably the hard
part, understanding that,

00:01:25.126 --> 00:01:28.004
but, you know, now it's like a next step

00:01:28.004 --> 00:01:31.174
and try to take control of your journey.

00:01:31.174 --> 00:01:34.135
- We've always seen you
be real calm and cool.

00:01:34.135 --> 00:01:35.887
I think it's only been a few times

00:01:35.887 --> 00:01:37.305
I've actually seen you show that emotion.

00:01:37.305 --> 00:01:40.308
What makes you so calm
out there and stay even-keeled?

00:01:40.308 --> 00:01:42.644
- Yeah, I don't know how I level it out, honestly.

00:01:42.644 --> 00:01:49.943
Cuz on the court, it's like, it's all a go.
It's like whatever, it's whatever.

00:01:49.943 --> 00:01:53.029
But my face never kinda change,
but my attitude is like,

00:01:53.029 --> 00:01:56.366
it's definitely, you know,
kill at all time.

00:01:56.366 --> 00:01:57.826
- I think the only times we've seen you

00:01:57.826 --> 00:01:58.868
with that emotion was

00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:00.662
versus St. Patrick's a couple years ago.

00:02:00.662 --> 00:02:02.664
If you remember, the scene was crazy,

00:02:02.664 --> 00:02:04.749
the crowd was going really nuts.

00:02:04.749 --> 00:02:07.210
And I saw that emotion out of
you. You turned to the crowd,

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:09.337
it was very, like, animated and whatnot.

00:02:09.337 --> 00:02:11.589
You know, what was that game like?

00:02:11.589 --> 00:02:13.133
- Yeah, just being back, you know,

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:14.425
where it kinda all started,

00:02:14.425 --> 00:02:18.429
in Jersey, all my family from Jersey.

00:02:18.429 --> 00:02:19.597
A lot of them still live there.

00:02:19.597 --> 00:02:23.601
So being at where it's all
started from,

00:02:23.601 --> 00:02:25.395
being that I was coming back,

00:02:25.395 --> 00:02:26.729
playing against a team

00:02:26.729 --> 00:02:28.356
that's in the middle of Jersey.

00:02:28.356 --> 00:02:33.361
The crowd was crazy.
They talking trash.

00:02:33.611 --> 00:02:36.406
I haven't been like that in a long time.

00:02:36.406 --> 00:02:38.575
Like just, you know, emotion like that.

00:02:38.575 --> 00:02:41.161
But it was like, they was so on the court,

00:02:41.161 --> 00:02:42.412
like you could hear everything.

00:02:42.412 --> 00:02:43.746
So when I'm hearing everything,

00:02:43.746 --> 00:02:45.832
just mainly from this side of this crowd,

00:02:45.832 --> 00:02:48.960
so you make a couple of shots,
it's like, what?

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.336
Like, why y'all not talking?

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:52.338
So that's all it was.

00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.465
It was definitely a fun experience, though.

00:02:54.465 --> 00:02:56.551
- Yeah. You've mentioned some of the,

00:02:56.551 --> 00:02:59.220
some players that you really, like,
model your game after,

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:00.722
look up to.

00:03:00.722 --> 00:03:03.641
And it seems like CP is like, a first.

00:03:03.641 --> 00:03:05.768
You know, Chris Paul.

00:03:05.768 --> 00:03:06.936
What is it you take from him

00:03:06.936 --> 00:03:08.104
and what is y'all relationship like?

00:03:08.104 --> 00:03:10.648
- What I take from him, just the way

00:03:10.648 --> 00:03:12.901
he can balance passing and scoring.

00:03:12.901 --> 00:03:15.403
I never felt like I was a
scoring or a pass first.

00:03:15.403 --> 00:03:18.198
I felt like I could do a
mix of both at a high level.

00:03:18.198 --> 00:03:20.992
So that's one person I really
see that, you know,

00:03:20.992 --> 00:03:24.245
could mix both and still be effective.

00:03:24.245 --> 00:03:26.372
So just watching him throughout the years,

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:28.041
the whole time I grew up.

00:03:28.041 --> 00:03:29.209
That's somebody I kind of see myself similar to.

00:03:29.209 --> 00:03:32.545
I'm not jumping over the rim,

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:33.880
not doing all that,

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.674
but you always is one of the
best players on the floor.

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:39.427
So that's somebody I look up to.
And we had a great relationship

00:03:39.427 --> 00:03:42.347
ever since we played their team in eighth grade

00:03:42.347 --> 00:03:46.267
and he was there and we lost,
and I actually had a good game,

00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:47.268
but you know,

00:03:47.268 --> 00:03:48.937
he kinda respected my game.

00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:51.856
He seen me crying
and he [chuckling]—he's like,

00:03:51.856 --> 00:03:54.359
"show me," he said,

00:03:54.359 --> 00:03:57.320
"Show me somebody who like losing,
and I'll show you a loser."

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:58.905
That's something he says,

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:01.115
and that's what he told me after the game.

00:04:01.115 --> 00:04:02.492
So we just had a good relationship

00:04:02.492 --> 00:04:03.326
ever since then.

00:04:03.326 --> 00:04:05.245
- What is your leadership style?

00:04:05.245 --> 00:04:07.455
Like, if you can describe it?

00:04:07.455 --> 00:04:09.582
- I want to win at all costs.

00:04:09.582 --> 00:04:11.626
I feel like I have a good understanding

00:04:11.626 --> 00:04:13.503
of people and my teammates.

00:04:13.503 --> 00:04:16.172
I always had a great
relationship with everybody.

00:04:16.172 --> 00:04:21.052
So I know how to talk to
certain people a certain way.

00:04:21.052 --> 00:04:23.721
I know who can take yelling and who can't.

00:04:23.721 --> 00:04:27.767
So everything I do is kind of
based off when it's,

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:30.520
whatever I say come from a good heart.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:33.314
My team know that.
I don't really do too much yelling,

00:04:33.314 --> 00:04:37.110
but, you know, when I say something,

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:37.944
it's mutual respect.

00:04:37.944 --> 00:04:39.696
- Did you, I mean, even draft night,

00:04:39.696 --> 00:04:44.325
I wanted to ask you about.
Like, what was that night like

00:04:44.325 --> 00:04:46.286
in the sense of being so,
waiting so long?

00:04:46.286 --> 00:04:47.328
We talked about patience earlier

00:04:47.328 --> 00:04:49.122
and waiting to hear your name

00:04:49.122 --> 00:04:50.206
and that relief that

00:04:50.206 --> 00:04:51.040
came out your face.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:52.583
But like, what was the first thing that popped

00:04:52.583 --> 00:04:54.127
in your mind after you
finally heard your name

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:55.128
that's like, all right.

00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:56.212
- It was hard, honestly.

00:04:56.212 --> 00:04:58.464
Cuz you know, when I came out of college

00:04:58.464 --> 00:04:59.757
in the projected lottery,
this, this and that,

00:04:59.757 --> 00:05:02.302
all the way over to the combine,

00:05:02.302 --> 00:05:04.053
they like, you gon' be a top-20,

00:05:04.053 --> 00:05:05.305
whatever, whatever.

00:05:05.305 --> 00:05:08.308
So I had great workouts, great meetings.

00:05:08.308 --> 00:05:09.100
People, you know,

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.936
calling me all the way
up until the draft date.

00:05:11.936 --> 00:05:12.770
So when I get there

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:14.355
and it's like, aight, I'm still waiting,

00:05:14.355 --> 00:05:15.189
still waiting.

00:05:15.189 --> 00:05:17.692
It's like, the way I was waiting

00:05:17.692 --> 00:05:19.485
for that long it's like, aight,

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:21.154
you gon' pay for that.

00:05:21.154 --> 00:05:23.406
You gon' pay for that for sure.

00:05:23.406 --> 00:05:24.949
- Yeah. Did you, I mean,

00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:26.242
I know that the chip on your shoulder,

00:05:26.242 --> 00:05:27.076
I'm sure it's huge now.

00:05:27.076 --> 00:05:28.578
We're coming into the season

00:05:28.578 --> 00:05:29.495
and the goals that you have

00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:30.330
set out for yourself.

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:31.956
What do you have set out to accomplish?

00:05:31.956 --> 00:05:34.500
Let's just say rookie year, year one.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:36.836
- I don't know.
There's so much up in the air.

00:05:37.712 --> 00:05:39.422
I just want to, you know,

00:05:39.422 --> 00:05:40.923
go in to practice the next day

00:05:40.923 --> 00:05:43.176
and just, you know, dominate.

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:44.886
I feel I got a lot to show,

00:05:44.886 --> 00:05:47.347
stuff that I haven't shown yet,

00:05:47.347 --> 00:05:48.973
that I feel like I show all the time on,

00:05:48.973 --> 00:05:50.933
outside of the camera.

00:05:50.933 --> 00:05:53.686
So I'm just ready for practice, man.

00:05:53.686 --> 00:05:55.271
That's it. I'm ready for the

00:05:55.271 --> 00:05:57.357
next practice, training camp.

00:05:57.357 --> 00:05:58.900
And then after that, the next practice.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.069
So that's all my mind is set on.

00:06:01.069 --> 00:06:03.571
The games,
I haven't even experienced the games,

00:06:03.571 --> 00:06:04.739
so I don't know what it's like.

00:06:04.739 --> 00:06:07.367
So I'm not really worried
about that right now.

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:08.159
- Yeah.

00:06:08.159 --> 00:06:10.161
Did you feel like you showed a good arsenal

00:06:10.161 --> 00:06:11.662
of your game in summer league?

00:06:11.662 --> 00:06:13.456
Summer league was like, really exciting

00:06:13.456 --> 00:06:15.249
to see how you running the offense,

00:06:15.249 --> 00:06:16.376
you and Jalen [Johnson],

00:06:16.376 --> 00:06:18.169
the way you guys kinda
handle things together,

00:06:18.169 --> 00:06:19.379
the game-winning shot you had.

00:06:19.379 --> 00:06:20.213
What do you think

00:06:20.213 --> 00:06:22.423
you were able to show summer league this past—

00:06:22.423 --> 00:06:25.551
- I feel like I showed it a little bit.

00:06:25.551 --> 00:06:27.095
Nothing to what I think

00:06:27.095 --> 00:06:29.555
I can show.

00:06:29.555 --> 00:06:31.099
I get better off playing five-on-five

00:06:31.099 --> 00:06:32.558
and stuff like that.

00:06:32.558 --> 00:06:34.852
So that's kind of how I develop a rhythm.

00:06:34.852 --> 00:06:36.896
I've been playing a lot of five-on-five

00:06:36.896 --> 00:06:37.730
after the summer league,

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:40.316
so I feel like I'm in a
great rhythm right now.

00:06:40.316 --> 00:06:41.692
I did all right.

00:06:41.692 --> 00:06:44.821
I think I'd give myself a C, C-plus

00:06:44.821 --> 00:06:46.781
for what I did.

00:06:46.781 --> 00:06:49.700
- You have a very strong fanbase.

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:51.828
I don't know if you're aware of that [laughing],

00:06:51.828 --> 00:06:53.788
but they'll do anything for you.

00:06:53.788 --> 00:06:54.831
What is it like

00:06:54.831 --> 00:06:56.165
just having that support system,

00:06:56.165 --> 00:06:59.252
you know, fans that really
ride with you constantly?

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:00.878
And it's like, yo, if we,

00:07:00.878 --> 00:07:03.214
if they see a top 10 of point guards,

00:07:03.214 --> 00:07:04.048
if they see a list and

00:07:04.048 --> 00:07:04.882
it's like, oh, where's Sharife?

00:07:04.882 --> 00:07:06.884
It's quickly being asked,

00:07:06.884 --> 00:07:07.718
where's Sharife at?

00:07:07.718 --> 00:07:08.553
But they really ride for you.

00:07:08.553 --> 00:07:09.429
What is that like?

00:07:09.429 --> 00:07:10.221
- It's dope.

00:07:10.221 --> 00:07:13.558
It's great to have a support staff outside,

00:07:13.558 --> 00:07:16.936
you know, of your, of
your family and anything.

00:07:16.936 --> 00:07:19.772
I see the same stuff you see.

00:07:19.772 --> 00:07:22.608
I mean, they always got my back.

00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:24.902
When somebody say
something that's not true

00:07:24.902 --> 00:07:26.446
or whatever the case may be,

00:07:26.446 --> 00:07:28.072
they always the first one to respond.

00:07:28.072 --> 00:07:29.073
So, you know,

00:07:29.073 --> 00:07:31.075
I'm super thankful for them, for sure.

00:07:31.075 --> 00:07:33.119
- If somebody asks you right now,

00:07:33.119 --> 00:07:35.121
which one you gotta claim,
is it Jersey or is it Atlanta?

00:07:35.121 --> 00:07:37.832
- [Laughing]
- And why too?

00:07:38.833 --> 00:07:41.210
- I have to go with both.

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:44.881
I feel like I'm from my roots,

00:07:44.881 --> 00:07:46.132
you know, my heart, my blood

00:07:46.132 --> 00:07:47.341
is in Jersey.

00:07:47.341 --> 00:07:50.178
But you know, I grew up in Georgia,

00:07:50.178 --> 00:07:52.972
so I always gon' have that, you know,

00:07:52.972 --> 00:07:53.806
tie to both of them.

00:07:53.806 --> 00:07:57.185
But definitely, I probably say Jersey.

00:07:57.185 --> 00:07:59.395
I love Atlanta though, still.

00:07:59.395 --> 00:08:00.646
I grew up here, you know, ATL.

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:02.607
That's what I got on my jersey.
- Yeah.

00:08:02.607 --> 00:08:04.650
- So that's who we rockin' with.
♪♪♪

00:08:04.650 --> 00:08:04.692
So that's who we rocking with.

